OCCUPY by Noam Chomsky

2012. Penguin Special. Paperback.

This book is almost the same size and format as another left-wing
Penguin I've read and reviewed, "Things that can and cannot be said"
with John Cusack, Arundathi Roy, Edward Snowden and Daniel Ellsberg.
That book I read and offloaded; this book (by Noam Chomsky, a legend
of left-wing thought and activism) I'll probably keep and revisit. If
I do end up tossing it, I'll update this review.

Occupy Wall Street. Occupy Chicago. Occupy Boston. What was this
political movement all about? They didn't seem to have demands and
seemed to vanish in the embarassment of issues of a
community-leadership tent city (stealing and rapes and attacks between
the protestors themselves).

Chomsky explains the movement is the result of class warfare that's
been going since the 1070s. Ten percent of the top one percent of
people have all the money and power, while the rest of us (the 99
percent) have stagnant wages and have to work overtime or second jobs
just to stay in the same place. While not poverty of third-world
proportions, this shouldn't be the case in a wealthy country like the
United States.

Like "Things that can/cannot be said", "Occupy" is in small sections
of 20 pages or so, here a Boston speech, a few interviews at
Universities and (a very nice inclusion) an appreciation of another
legend of political protest, Howard Zinn. There's a little duplication
of thought across the interviews, the editor or Chomsky himself
reminds us of this with "as I said in my speech at Boston" and the
duplication helps remind the reader and emphasize the point.

A right-wing movement around at the same time, also with ill-defined
aims and demands, was the Tea Party movement. It seems to have been
mostly an anti-Obama coalition and was absorbed into MAGA and
Republixan voters generally.

Money in politics is a huge problem that Chomsky rails against. He
doesn't despair though; priveleged white Americans have the power to
organise, to demonstrate and to agitate and every bit helps. The
community want health care and a decent life; so explaining the
problems and listening to their concerns and building a movement from
the grassroots can affect real change; the same as it did for civil
rights in the 1960s, anti-War in the Vietnam period and also in the
was against Iraq and women's rights.

I am by no means a left-winger and normally read these left-wing
Penguin Specials from a curiosity to "see how the other half
think".Things that cannot be said and Kurt Vonnegut novels haven't
convinced me, and I owned and hoicked a Penguin "Extinction Rebellion"
handbook, but while not changing my vote, Chomsky has got me
thinking. I have been involved with another MIT left-wing radical,
Free Software pioneer Richard Stallman (who slept under this desk and
spent 168 hours a week on campus for months at a time while writing
the free software GNU replacements for UNIX software),

Is the occupy movement finished? Yes, but getting the "we are the 99
percent" phrase into popular attention and newspaper articles was a
win. Interestingly the count of the number of protestors arrested is
used in the book as a measure of the movement's success and there's a
small section from lawyers telling protestors what to do if arrested.

Like "Timequake" I took this book with me on a Sunday away from home,
and had more than enough reading time to finish it in one day (it was
probably an eighty minute read, spread across four sessions). The
activities were a Thai Harbour Cruise and a Thai pop concert; both
interesting enough for my full attention, so the book was read in
twenty minute waiting around periods before those activities. Reading
a book in this way is much better than checking my phone all day.

